From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Date: Sat, 05 Mar 94 23:20:16 GMT
Subject: [#94-3-65] Natural Rights and Ancient Liberties

Tim Starr replies:
>>You mention "negative" rights so does this exclude the right to a free
>>data super-highway?
>
>Nope.  That would be a positive claim right.

I think you read my "exclude" as "include", we seem to agree that "right 
to a free data highway" is a fiction or an unnatural fabrication. 

>So, we're caught in the curious position in which one camp insists that
>negative rights are fictions, as opposed to positive ones which are
>objective, and another camp holds precisely the opposite.  This
>compounds the usual difficulty in the discovery and enforcement of rights.

Yes, the former camp, the pro-statists, can be seen to have the less
credible position, since new positive claim rights are constantly being
invented.  I wanted to see how natural rights people would specify how to
tell a natural right from a fictitious concoction:

>As the Rasmussen and Den Uyl
>point out in "Liberty and Nature," the two characteristics of natural rights
>are negativity and universality.  Negativity means the right not to be
>interfered with, not the right to force others to provide you with any goods
>or services.  Universality means it applies to everybody.

And examples of sub-groups of rights which may be derived from negativity:

>...the right to freedom of
>speech/expression, and...the right to property.

This helps in most cases but I suspect there's a problem with
externalities -- e.g. if I own a smoky factory do I have the right to
pollute the air, or if an oil tanker do I have the right to unleash a
slick?  For the natural rights to be truly objective they should be as
readily distinguishable from fictions as odd from even numbers.

The statists' power is enhanced through persuading people to see positive
pseudo-rights as objective, and ancient negative rights and liberties as
the talk of capitalist dogs.  Actually, the British "Bill of Rights", a
1689 Act, reaffirmed the ancient rights and liberties of the nation, e.g.
Protestant subjects had the right to bear arms for their defence.  But
supposing we have a goal-based or ancient liberties interpretation of
natural rights:

(i)  When humans are free to pursue their own chosen goals, they are led
by "an invisible hand", a spontaneous order, to promote unintended ends,
which are frequently in the best interests of human society.

Natural rights seems to go like this:

(ii)  A set of objective natural rights exist, inherent in the nature of
mankind, which following discovery will, if enforced, increase general
prosperity.  The two characteristics of natural rights are negativity and
universality.  Negativity means the right not to be interfered with, as
opposed to positivity, which is the right to force others to provide you
with goods or services.  An infinite number of rights are awaiting
discovery.  Universality means these rights apply to everybody.

It looks like (ii) is just a more longwinded way of saying (i), which is
already known by supporters of free markets.  (Although historically, 
knowledge of natural rights preceded Adam Smith, for instance.)  Or is
there something more to natural rights which would yield differing
predictions?

Examples cited as evidence for interpretation (i) could be 19th century
USA and Britain, and 20th century Hong Kong.  Also cf. with Russia,
Bosnia, and India nowadays or cf. former West vs. East Germany.

One advantage of using a rights-based description would be that most
people have been conditioned into thinking in terms of rights ever since
the statists hijacked the concept for their own purposes.  Citizens have
also been encouraged to mistrust freedom and "unbridled capitalism".

Liberty and rights could be combined: point out that liberty and natural
rights are good for people but not for statists / power sponges, so it's
in the interests of the people to enforce their natural rights and win
back their ancient liberties.

>>If rights were never enforced or respected there would be no evidence for
>>existence or pre-existence;
>
>Why not?  What does enforcement have to do with the evidence for them?

You gave the example of the Dutch becoming prosperous after enforcing 
natural rights, which could possibly be used as evidence.  If human nature
may be such that a set of natural rights exist which promote prosperity if
enforced or respected, how could this be falsified if rights were never
enforced or respected?  If I claimed that a genie existed who could
dramatically increase human prosperity if he wanted to, critics would
remark: "How convenient it is for the theory that he never chooses to!"

>>Construction
>>implies design, creation may be with or without intent. 
>
>I'm afraid "creation" really does entail intent.  Hayek suggests "forma-
>tion," but I'm not quite satisfied with it, either.

My dictionary has "give rise to" as one definition of 'create'.  And one
listing for 'rise' is "originate".  So I think 'create' could be Darwin-
approved, notwithstanding any Creator connotations.  Quite a range of
interpretation.  Is "Nature's creation" an oxymoron?  At worst I'd see it
as similar to a black swan, nothing like a black light.

>>Hardly
>>anyone even thinks about enforcing their right to non-coercion or right
>>to life, and statists certainly don't respect these.
>
>This is an issue of abstraction.  Since most people don't think abstractly
>about these things, but concretely, they think of concrete rights.

We have to find some way of encouraging abstract thinking, and show how
intangible concepts can lead to tangible benefits.

>>  If the theory
>>implies that rights will increasingly be enforced as they're discovered,
>>I hope it turns out to be true. 
>
>Sort of.  The discoveries have to be acted upon, too, and that's not always
>possible.  For instance, natural rights were known to the Scholastics during
>the Crusades in great clarity and detail, but little use was made of this
>knowledge until the Dutch broke free of the Spanish Empire - proceeding to
>become one of the most prosperous societies in the world upon the enforcement
>of natural rights as they understood them.

Was this during the 1590s or after 1648?  My encyclopedia mentions how the
Dutch economy blossomed during the 1590s after the Spanish had been
defeated in many sea battles and were forced to call a truce.  But it
wasn't until 1648 that the Netherlands' independence was recognised in the
Treaty of Munster.  Reasons given for the 1590s prosperity are the Baltic
trade and herring fishery.  If they were able to enforce natural rights
over this period it would support the theory, also if things worked out
well in the late 1600s.  They did have a war to fight (with the British
as allies) against the French, but in 1713 the Treaty of Utrecht was said
to have concluded the war and also the period of the Netherlands'
greatness as a world power.

