From: LEVY%BESSIE@venus.cis.yale.edu
Date: Sun, 06 Mar 1994 02:38:32 -0500 (EST)
Subject: [#94-3-69] ANTHRO: Multiple origins of modern homo-sapien

Thanks, Mike for the excellent summary on speciation in the genus homo.
Most of it was new information for me.  I agree with you, also, that it
seems strange that homo sapiens would have developed in parallel from 
different sub-groups of homo erectus. Being an ignoramus about physical
anthropology, I swallowed the _Times_ article whole. What a mistake!

> BTW, Simon!, that was interesting stuff about language.  Perhaps you
> could comment on something which puzzles me.  The genetic race map of
> homo-sapiens (us!) lineages seem similar to the super-family tree of
> languages.  But the diversification timescales are different by an order
> of magnitude - 100,000s versus 10,000s years antiquity.  It's early days
> in both depratments, I know, but am I seeing imaginary patterns or could
> the old languages have evolved much more slowly than we presently think?

I'm not sure what you mean by the "super-family tree of languages".  Perhaps
you are referring to the all-embracing families like Nostratic that some
linguists (e.g., Joseph Greenberg) have been arguing for recently.  Most
linguists consider such groupings far-fetched, and are much more comfortable
putting up with the well-established system of several unrelated language
groups.  (Though Greenberg points out that he got just as much flak years
ago when he was trying to establish one of the these groups, which is now
universally accepted)  The extent to which such groups correlate with race 
varies a great deal; for example, the Japanese and Chinese are racially 
similar, but their two languages are unrelated to one another genetically 
(though there has been heavy borrowing from Chinese to Japanese).  Conversely,
you find people of many different races speaking English nowadays.

As for the speed with which languages evolved, I doubt that it has changed
much over time.  In fact, a linguist named Morris Swadesh once computed
a constant that was supposed to tell you how many years ago two related
languages diverged, based on their present similarity.  No one ever took
the formula very seriously, but the fact that a well-known scholar proposed
it in the first place shows the extent to which a constant rate of change
is taken for granted in the historical linguistics community.  BTW, literacy 
tends to slow down the rate of change, so it would be reasonable to say that 
languages  have been changing more *slowly* in recent times.


-- Simon!

