From: carlf@media.mit.edu (Carl Feynman)
Date: Sun, 6 Mar 1994 15:44:54 -0100
Subject: [#94-3-71] SCI: Femtotech

A while ago I said:

> my depressing conclusion after looking into the possiblilty of
> strangelet-based femtotech: nuclear matter, strange or not, is 
> always liquid.

When I was involved in the discussion of this matter last year, I spent a
while poking around in libraries and found no mention of solid states
observed in ordinary nuclear matter, or predicted for strange nuclear
matter.  This is how       cam eto the above conclusion.  Unfortunately the
messages that I sent to the list on this matter are no longer available to
me.  If anyone would like to ressurect them from old archive files, that's
fine by me.

I've gotten a couple of off-list messages asking for more on this topic.  I
didn't feel like going to the library, so I tried thinking instead.  I did
a few calculations with depressing results.  Here's what I did; I hope that
physicists on the list can check me.  It seems like if we were going to
build things out of nuclear matter, we could have to confine nucleons to
positions on the order of their own radii, so just as we confine atoms to
positions within 10^-10m for nanotech, we would need to confine nucleons to
within 10^-15 m.  The uncertainty principle says that something with a
position uncertainty of 10^-15m has a momentum uncertainty of p=1.05E-19 kg
m/s.  The kinetic energy is (1/2) p^2/m, or 20.72 MeV, where m is the mass
of a proton (hydrogen atom, actually, but that doesn't matter).  

20 MeV is a lot.  The maximum binding energy of a nucleon to a nucleus is
only about 7 MeV.  So it's very hard to see how you could confine nucleons
to any particular position.  In actual nuclei, they are smeared all over
the nucleus, just as individual electrons are smeared all over an atom.

The possibly wrong bits of the above calculation are (a) the assumption
that the volume per nucleon is 1 fm^3 (I was unable to find the density of
a typical nucleus.  The 1 fm number is from a study of neutron star
matter.) and (b) I don't know much quantum mechanics, so my calculation of
the zero-point energy might be wrong by some factors of 2.  

Mr. Price suggested that perhaps nuclear fluids might be viscous enough to
build things out of, like glass or asphalt are for ordinary technology.  It
seems to me that tunneling to lower energy states would be the main
mechanism for relaxaton of shapes.  One could get a feel for this by
looking at the halflife of nuclear isomers, but I don't know what these
typically are, and finding out would require me to get out of my desk
chair, so I'll leave that up to someone else.

Moravec suggests that femtotech would be possible only if there was a
stable negatively charged particle more massive than the electron.  Unless
we find such a thing, I think we're stuck with mere nanotech.


--carlf

