From: Michael Clive Price <price@price.demon.co.uk>
Date: Mon, 07 Mar 94 12:25:21 GMT
Subject: [#94-3-78] BIO: Codons and amino acids

More random thoughts, of dubious relevance....

A little item in New Scientist caught my eye.  Some clever folks have
reconstructed, using arcane group theory, the evolution of the codon =>
amino acid mapping.

To recap, the DNA helix is composed of 4 different nucleotide bases,
denoted A, C, G & T, with an implied grouping into 4^3 = 64 triplets or
codons "visible" to the gene expression mechanism (RNA and ribosomes). 
Only 20 amino acids types are used as building blocks for proteins, each
amino acid in a protein sequence being specified by a codon in the DNA. 
There are over a 100 amino acids that don't occur in proteins, yet the
DNA only knows of 20, far short of the potential 63 it could code cope
with, implying an average three-fold redundancy in the DNA codons.  For
instance the six triplets GCA, GCG, GCT, GCC, TCT and TCC all code for
the *same* amino acid, arginine, whereas only ATG codes for one amino
acid, methionine.  Three codons (ATT, ATC & ACT) don't code for any
amino acid but indicate to the ribosome, via RNA, the end (and/or
start?) of a protein encoding sequence.  In terms of compression the DNA
not an efficient code.

The thrust of the recent work is to attack the history of the codon =>
amino acid mapping with trendy group theory.  Different codons that
produce the same amino acid are treated as a symmetry (or degeneracy). 
The idea is that long, long ago the 64 codons coded for fewer different
amino acids - we could say that the symmetry was higher and less broken
than it is now - and the symmetry has been progressively broken as
evolution has slotted new amino acids into the 64 entry table.  This way
the proliferation of amino acids types forms an evolutionary family tree
structure.  

Drawing on the extensive work done with groups and symmetries these
folks have established that one tree structure is much more probable
than any other - something to do with the way a particular group
uniquely decomposes into sub-groups blah, blah - mercifully, we are
spared the details in NS :-).  In their reconstruction there were only
6 different amino acids to "start with".  No indication of timescale is
given (or known).  The 6 original amino acids have split into the 20 we
have today.

My question is, do all living organisms share the same codon/amino acid
mapping?  Do trees, say, have the same amino acids in their proteins as
we do?  My guess is that they do, although my books are vague on the
subject, since otherwise the first step in a reconstruction would have
been, surely, to do a comparative study.  Assuming, then, uniformity of
expression, the present mapping, with its still high degree of symmetry
and redundancy, has remained unaltered since at least the Pre-Cambrian? 
If so, why?  Could it be that there is something special and unique
about the mapping necessary for multicellular life, some threshold of
complexity that, once crossed, enabled complex life forms to evolve (cf
the Cambrian "explosion" of approx 600 million years ago)?  That the
earlier <20-amino-based life was so relativily inefficient that it was
all wiped out?

