From: pavel@PARK.BU.EDU (Paul Cisek)
Date: Tue, 8 Mar 94 12:55:39 -0500
Subject: [#94-3-81] BIO: Codons and amino acids

Mike Price describes (X-message: #94-3-78) very interesting research on the
evolution of the codon => amino acid used in DNA transcription.  I am intrigued
to learn that the code is theorized to have evolved, though of course it could
not have just popped into place.  As far as my surface knowledge of genetics
recalls, the mRNA strand copied from the DNA is carried to the ribosomes where
it is translated into proteins by tRNA molecules.  The tRNA molecules consist
of a codon of three nucleotides and a site that binds with a specific amino
acid.  Thus when the tRNA arrange along the mRNA strand they cause the amino
acids to be arranged in the proper order.  Anyway, so much for my review of
protein synthesis...

Why would any mapping prevail over any other?  I suppose it could just be a
matter of chance, depending only on what tRNA molecules happened to develop.
Perhaps there were initially only a few types of tRNA, which later
differentiated into the 20+ we see today.  (A related question: how are the
tRNA molecules synthesized?  Are the required catalysts also proteins?  Are the
older amino acids coded by tRNA which are synthesized through less logically
deep enzymes? ...).

But anyway, here's an extropian idea I heard about on internet once.  Let's
suppose that we can design our own tRNA molecules (through clever
biotechnology, or perhaps nanotech), and thus can choose an arbitrary codon =>
amino acid mapping.  Let's also suppose that we have specialized `cRNA' that
bind with the chromosomes during cell division and shuffle the nucleotides in
the copied DNA strand.  Well, then we could have a mechanism for DNA
encryption.  A fertilized egg could be injected with the `cryptoRNA' and a set
of appropriate tRNA, and then allowed to divide.  The new cell would now have
an encrypted DNA code and a ribosome-tRNA mechanism able to decode it into
appropriate proteins.  The result is an organism that is completely immune to
viruses of any sort.

Anyway, it's farfetched, but I thought it's quite an interesting idea when I
heard it (forgot the author though...).

Paul


